 "description": "Task 1054 review follow-up (NEW COVERAGE) -- band mode at count > 1. The Phase-3 review found every case in the 54-case corpus, both new fixtures, and both interactive tests used exactly ONE cut position, so the multi-position paths in band mode (the middle-quad loop and the multi-mid absorb splice) were entirely unexercised. The review's hypothesis was that this ALSO explains why a one-site inversion of the entry/exit convention (R1) was silently absorbed everywhere; that hypothesis was tested against this fixture by mutation (both a one-site swap -- entry-population loop only -- and a full swap of both assignment sites) and did NOT redden either way, for a reason unrelated to position count: the five-cell L selection used here (same as tests/fixtures/loop_slice_corner.json, row-first click order) is symmetric under a GLOBAL entry/exit reversal of the whole chain -- reversing which side of every cell is called entry vs exit is equivalent to walking the same band from its other end, which produces the identical cut. `loop_slice_band.json`'s own `L_p3_cornerfirst` case (a DIFFERENT selection order, pos=0.3) is what actually discriminates a full swap; no case at count > 1 exists in the captured corpus to test a differently-ordered selection. This fixture's real, verified value is exercising the previously-DEAD multi-position code paths (the middle-quad loop and the multi-mid absorb splice both run for the first time under a real assertion here) -- see also test_fixture_loop_slice_band_count.d's own header comment. Same 3x1x3 grid and same five-cell L as tests/fixtures/loop_slice_corner.json, uniform mode, count 2 and 3 (positions computed by our own i/(count+1) law, matching the reference's -- plan section 1 point 6). Every expected_after/expected_vertices/expected_face_degrees number is reference-sourced (tools/local/fixture_gen/loop_slice_band/count_gt1/bandcount.json), never read off vibe3d's own output.",
